# DRIVE UI Rules

- Before UI work, read `docs/00-product-brief.md`, `docs/02-game-design-rules.md`, and `docs/04-user-roles-and-safeguarding.md`.
- Use a serious but fun junior rowing tone. Avoid childish gimmicks, empty hype, and generic fitness app language.
- Do not use purple or blue gradients, or purple/blue as the primary colour direction.
- Screens must stay UI only. Move business logic, verification logic, Firebase access, and reward decisions out of screens.
- UI should reinforce execution quality: pacing discipline, rate control, reflection, honest uploads, and coach verified effort.
- Do not create pure speed leaderboard UI unless explicitly approved.
- Do not add public athlete profiles, public rankings, social feeds, direct messaging, or unnecessary junior athlete data collection without explicit approval.
- Keep copy in UK English.
- After UI changes, summarise changed files and explain how the UI supports the DRIVE brief.
